s smaller than 2.828, which is inconsistent. Therefore, this is impossible.\n\nWait, this shows that s must be less than or equal to the diameter at the top layer\'s height. Therefore, the error arises here.\n\nAnother approach: The top layer of the cube is on the cone\'s surface, which at height z is a circle of radius r(z)=1 - z/3. The top layer is a square of side s, hence its diagonal is s√2. Therefore, the diagonal must fit within the cone\'s cross-section at height z, so s√2 = 2*(1 - h/3). So s=√2*(1 - h/3). On the other hand, the vertical edge is h, and the horizontal displacement is h/3. Since the bottom layer is centered at the origin, after moving h in vertical, the center is at (h/3,0, h). Therefore, the top layer is centered at (h/3,0, h). The vertices are then at (h/3 ± s/2, 0, h), but in cube coordinates, actually, the top layer is a square of side s aligned with the axes. Wait, but how does the cube\'s top layer relate to the cone\'s curvature? If the cube is axis-aligned, then the top layer\'s square would have vertices at (h/3 ± s/2, 0, h) and ( ± s/2, 0, h). Then, these points lie on the cone\'s surface. So plugging into the cone\'s equation z =3 - sqrt(x2 + y2):\n\nh =3 - sqrt( (h/3 ± s/2)^2 + (0)^2 )\n\nTherefore,\n\nh =3 - |h/3 ± s/2 |\n\nSince h <3,\n\nh + |h/3 ± s/2 | =3\n\nAs h is positive and less than3,\n\n| h/3 ± s/2 | =3 - h\n\nThus,\n\nh/3 ± s/2 ≤0 ?\n\nWait, no, since h <3 and s must be positive, then h/3 + s/2 >0 and h/3 -s/2 could be positive or negative. But since sqrt term must be <= h/3 + s/2, but here we have |something|=3 -h, so h/3 ± s/2 must both be <=3 -h. Therefore,\n\nh/3 ± s/2 =3 -h\n\nTherefore, we have two possible equations:\n\nh/3 + s/2 =3 -h --> s/2=3 -h -h/3=(9 -3h -h)/3=(9 -4h)/3 -->s=2*(9 -4h)/3=(18 -8h)/3=6 - (8/3)h\n\nor\n\nh/3 -s/2=3 -h --> -s/2=3 -h -h/3=(9 -3h -h)/3=(9 -4h)/3 -->s/2= - (9 -4h)/3=(4h -9)/3 -->s=2*(4h -9)/3=(8h -18)/3\n\nBut since s must be positive, we have to check which solution is valid.\n\nCase1: s=6 - (8/3)h\n\nWe need 6 - (8/3)h >0 -->h< (6*3)/8=9/4=2.25\n\nCase2: s=(8h -18)/3\n\nNeed (8h -18)/3 >0 -->8h>18 -->h>2.25\n\nBut if h>2.25, then first equation gives s negative, which is impossible. Thus, only Case1 is possible, so s=6 - (8/3)h, with h <2.25. But we also have from previous equation s=√2*(1 -h/3). So:\n\n6 - (8/3)h = sqrt(2)*(1 - h/3)\n\nRearrange:\n\n6 - (8/3)h - sqrt(2) + (sqrt(2)/3)h =0 --> common factor h:\n\nh*(-8/3 + sqrt(2)/3 )= -6 + (8/3)sqrt(2)\n\nFactor out (-1/3):\n\nh=( -6 + (8/3)sqrt(2) ) / (-1/3 + sqrt(2)/3 )\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n-6 + (8/3)sqrt(2)\n\nDenominator:\n\n(-1/3 + sqrt(2)/3 )= (-1 + sqrt(2))/3\n\nThus,\n\nh= [ -6 + (8/3)sqrt(2) ] / [ (-1 + sqrt(2)) /3 ]= [ -6 + (8/3)sqrt(2) ] * [3 / (sqrt(2) -1 ) ]\n\nMultiply:\n\n= [ -18 + 8sqrt(2) ] / (sqrt(2)-1 )\n\nRationalize denominator:\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by (sqrt(2)+1):\n\n[ (-18 +8sqrt(2))(sqrt(2)+1) ] / (2 -1 )= [ -18sqrt(2) -18 + 16 +8sqrt(2) ) ] = [ (-18sqrt(2)+8sqrt(2)) + (-18 +16) ]= [ -10sqrt(2) -2 ]\n\nThus,\n\nh= -10sqrt(2)-2 which is negative, which is impossible. Thus, contradiction.\n\nTherefore, our assumption that the cube can be expressed in terms of both equations is wrong. Hence, there must be an error in this approach.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is to note that the cube is inscribed in the cone such that four of its vertices are on the base (radius1, height0) and four on the cone’s side. The cube\'s edges are vertical, so these edges are moving from the base upwards along lines that are vertical in the cone. Since the cone\'s walls are 1 horizontal unit per 3 vertical units, moving up 1 unit, the horizontal position steps back 1/3 units. Each vertical edge of the cube has length s. So, if the cube is at height h, the net horizontal displacement due to stepping back must relate to the size s.\n\nBut if the cube is a unit cube, but no. Let me think.\n\nIf you move vertically up h, and each vertical step moves you back 1/3 in horizontal. So, the total horizontal reach of the cube is h*(1/3). But the base of the cube is a square of side s. The distance from the center to the base vertex is s/2. Therefore, h*(1/3) = s/2. Hence, h = (3/2)*(s). So, h = 3s/2.\n\nAlternatively, the ratio of vertical movement to horizontal displacement is 1 vertical : 1/3 horizontal. So, for each vertical edge of length s, the horizontal component is (1/3)*s. If the base is centered at the origin, the four bottom vertices are at (s/2, s/2,0), etc. The horizontal displacement after moving up h must be (1/3)*s*h. Therefore, in the x and y directions, each vertex is at (±s/2 ± (1/3)s h, ±s/2 ± (1/3)s h, h ). Wait, but this is getting too convoluted.\n\nAlternatively, consider that each vertical edge of the cube, moving a vertex from (s/2, s/2,0) to (X, Y, h), must satisfy the cone equation h =3 - sqrt(X2 + Y2). The distance moved is s: sqrt( (X -s/2)^2 + (Y -s/2)^2 + h2 )=s. So, we have two equations:\n\n1. sqrt( (X -s/2)^2 + (Y -s/2)^2 + h2 )=s\n\n2. h=3 - sqrt(X2 + Y2)\n\nAssuming symmetry, the simplest case is when the edge moves to the right in x and up in z. So, Y=s/2, so movement is only in x and z. Thus, (X, Y, h)= (s/2, s/2,0) + (Δx, 0, h). Then, X = s/2 + Δx, and sqrt(Δx2 + h2) =s. So,\n\nsqrt( (Δx)^2 + h^2 )=s --> Δx='}]}
